    "summary": "# Lecture #105: Pathology of Bone and Joints - Part 2\n\n**Lecturer:** E. Petcu, M.D. | **Session:** Lecture #105\n\nContinuation of [[L104]]. This lecture covers fractures, osteomyelitis, seronegative spondyloarthropathies, Lyme arthritis, fibrous dysplasia, bone cysts, and both benign and malignant bone tumors. The key theme: patient age, lesion location, and radiographic appearance are the strongest diagnostic clues.\n\n---\n\n## Fractures and Fracture Repair\n\n**Types:** Simple (closed, skin intact), compound (open, communicates with skin), comminuted (multiple fragments), displaced (ends not aligned), stress (repetitive loading), greenstick (incomplete, in infants), and pathological (through weakened bone, e.g. tumor).\n\n**Repair sequence:** Hematoma (fibrin mesh + growth factors PDGF/TGF-beta/FGF activate osteoprogenitor cells) --> Soft callus (pro-callus, provides anchorage but no rigidity) --> Hard callus (woven bone trabeculae, weeks 2-3) --> Endochondral ossification (mineralization, weight-bearing) --> Remodeling (restoration of medullary cavity). Inadequate immobilization --> non-union --> cystic degeneration --> **pseudoarthrosis** (false joint lined by synovial-like cells).\n\n:::highyield\nFracture repair: Hematoma --> Soft Callus --> Hard Callus --> Endochondral Ossification --> Remodeling. Non-union leads to pseudoarthrosis.\n:::\n\n---\n\n## Osteomyelitis\n\nInflammation of bone and marrow. S. aureus causes 80-90% of pyogenic cases. E. coli/Pseudomonas/Klebsiella in GU infections; H. influenzae/Group B Strep in neonates; **Salmonella in sickle cell disease**. Organisms reach bone hematogenously, by contiguous spread, or direct implantation.\n\n**Acute stage:** Neutrophil recruitment --> bone necrosis within 48 hours --> subperiosteal abscess --> periosteal lifting impairs blood supply. **Key terms:** Dead bone = **sequestrum**; reactive new bone shell = **involucrum**; draining sinus = **cloaca** (indicates chronicity). Chronic osteomyelitis in 5-25% of cases. X-ray: lytic focus surrounded by sclerosis.\n\n---\n\n## Seronegative Spondyloarthropathies\n\nDefined by: RF-negative, enthesopathy (not synovitis), sacroiliac involvement, HLA-B27 association, bony ankylosis.\n\n**Ankylosing spondylitis:** Bony ankylosis of sacroiliac and vertebral joints. Lower back pain + spinal immobility in 2nd-3rd decades. 90% HLA-B27 positive.\n\n**Reactive Arthritis (Reiter):** Triad of **arthritis + urethritis + conjunctivitis**. Triggered by Salmonella/Shigella/Campylobacter (GI) or Chlamydia trachomatis (STI). Acute asymmetric oligoarthritis of lower extremities. RF-negative, HLA-B27 positive. 50% recur; active disease beyond 6 months = chronic.\n\n**Lyme Arthritis:** *Borrelia burgdorferi* via *Ixodes* ticks. Three phases: (1) Early localized: erythema migrans (bull's-eye rash, round, red, expanding, non-pruritic); (2) Early disseminated: cranial nerves, heart, meninges; (3) Late disseminated: migratory arthritis in 80% untreated cases (large joints, 1-2 at a time). Arthritis occurs in less than 10% overall because most are treated early. Treatment: doxycycline/amoxicillin/cefuroxime axetil (90%+ cure).\n\n---\n\n## Fibrous Dysplasia\n\nBenign condition: normal bone components present but fail to mature. Caused by somatic **GNAS1** gain-of-function mutations (constitutively active Gs protein). Histology: curvilinear woven bone trabeculae (\"Chinese letters\") without osteoblastic rimming.\n\n| Form | Features |\n|---|---|\n| **Monostotic** | Adolescence; femur/tibia/ribs/jaw; often asymptomatic |\n| **Polyostotic** | Earlier age; craniofacial involvement; crippling deformities; rarely transforms to sarcoma |\n| **McCune-Albright** | Polyostotic (unilateral) + cafe-au-lait spots (ipsilateral) + precocious puberty + endocrinopathies |\n\n---\n\n## Bone Cysts\n\n- **Simple (unicameral):** Fluid-filled, fibrous-lined, first 2 decades, near growth plate. Not a neoplasm. May fracture if large.\n- **Aneurysmal (ABC):** Blood-filled, multiloculated. Adolescence; femur/tibia/vertebra. X-ray: expansive lytic lesion with sclerotic eggshell rim. 70% have USP6 rearrangements on 17p13. Benign but locally aggressive; 10-50% recurrence.\n\n**Ganglion cysts:** Small, near wrist joints, no cell lining. **Baker cyst:** Popliteal synovial cyst, associated with RA.\n\n---\n\n## Bone Tumors Overview\n\nPrimary bone tumors are rare and outnumbered by metastases. Most occur in the first 3 decades at the distal femur/proximal tibia. **Age is the most important diagnostic clue.** Painful lesions suggest malignancy.\n\n### Benign Tumors\n\n| Feature | Osteoid Osteoma | Osteoblastoma |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Size** | Less than 2 cm | Greater than 2 cm |\n| **Location** | Long bones | Spine |\n| **Pain** | Nocturnal (PGE2); **relieved by aspirin/NSAIDs** | NOT nocturnal; NOT aspirin-responsive |\n| **Treatment** | Radiofrequency ablation | Curettage/en bloc excision |\n\n:::highyield\nOsteoid osteoma: less than 2 cm, nocturnal pain relieved by aspirin (PGE2). Osteoblastoma: greater than 2 cm, spine, pain NOT relieved by aspirin.\n:::\n\n**Osteochondroma (exostosis):** Most common benign bone tumor. Bony stalk + cartilage cap. Metaphysis near growth plate (knee). 85% solitary. Men 3:1.\n\n**Enchondroma:** Benign hyaline cartilage tumor in medullary cavity. Ages 20-50. IDH1/IDH2 mutations. Ollier disease (unilateral enchondromas, 25-30% chondrosarcoma risk) and Maffucci syndrome (enchondromas + spindle cell hemangiomas, glioma risk).\n\n**Giant cell tumor (osteoclastoma):** Epiphysis near knee. X-ray: **soap-bubble appearance**. Giant cells with 100+ nuclei. 4% metastasize to lungs; 40-60% local recurrence.\n\n### Malignant Tumors\n\n**Osteosarcoma:** Most common primary malignant bone tumor (20% of bone cancers). Bimodal: 75% before age 20 (growth spurt); older adults with Paget/radiation. Metaphysis near knee. X-ray: **sunburst pattern** or **Codman triangle**. Diagnosis requires malignant cells producing osteoid. Genetics: RB (75% sporadic), TP53 (Li-Fraumeni), CDKN2A. All patients assumed to have occult metastasis. 5-year survival less than 20% with mets.\n\n**Ewing sarcoma:** Second most common pediatric bone sarcoma. 80% under 20. Caucasian predilection. Diaphysis of long bones/pelvis. **Mimics infection** (fever, elevated ESR, leukocytosis). X-ray: **onion-skin periosteal reaction**. Histology: small round blue cells forming **Homer Wright rosettes**. t(11;22) **EWSR1-FLI1** fusion in 90%+. Treatment: neoadjuvant chemo + surgery + adjuvant chemo; 75% 5-year survival.\n\n**Chondrosarcoma:** Malignant cartilage tumor. Age 40s+, men 2:1. Axial skeleton (pelvis/shoulder/ribs). Grade 1: 80-90% 5-year survival. Grade 3: 43% survival, 70% metastasize to lungs.\n\n---\n\n## Metastatic Bone Tumors\n\nFar more common than primary tumors. Adults: prostate (**blastic**), breast, kidney, lung (more than 75%). Kidney/lung/GI/melanoma = **lytic**. Breast = **mixed**. Children: neuroblastoma, Wilms, osteosarcoma, Ewing, rhabdomyosarcoma.\n\nTumor cells secrete PTHrP/prostaglandins --> upregulate RANKL --> stimulate osteoclast-driven resorption --> released growth factors (TGF-beta, IGF-1) feed back to support tumor growth (vicious cycle). Treatment: bisphosphonates (zoledronic acid), analgesics, radiotherapy.\n\n:::mnemonic\n**Primary Malignant Bone Tumors by Age (OEC):**\n**O**steosarcoma - under 20, metaphysis, sunburst/Codman\n**E**wing - children, diaphysis, onion-skin, t(11;22)\n**C**hondrosarcoma - adults 40+, axial skeleton\n:::",
